<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gandzasar Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1216">1216</a>–<a href="/years/1238">1238</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gandzasar is an Armenian monastery in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, built for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-jalal-dawla">Hasan-Jalal Dawla</a> of Khachen, whose Cathedral of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> was consecrated in 1240. It served for centuries as the seat of the Catholicos of Aghvank and the burial place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hasan-jalalyan">Hasan-Jalalyan</a> princes. Its carved stonework makes it one of the finest monuments of medieval Armenian architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gandzasar-monastery.jpg" alt="Gandzasar Monastery" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vahagn Grigoryan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DJI_-Gandzasar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
